Venus vGPU

This source distribution includes work from these projects and contributors.

Mesa and the Mesa Venus developers
  The Darwin Vulkan ICD is built from Mesa. The patches preserve Mesa's
  file-level notices and license terms, including the notices from Google,
  Collabora and other Mesa contributors. See the pinned Mesa source and
  its licenses directory for the complete notices.

Venus protocol and its contributors
  The driver-side protocol generator and templates come from the pinned
  Venus protocol repository. Its source files carry their original notices.

virglrenderer and its contributors, including Collabora and Red Hat
  The Linux renderer uses the UTM-maintained virglrenderer revision listed
  in docs/PROVENANCE.md. See its COPYING and source notices. The patches
  preserve the terms of the files they modify.

QEMU, UTM and Scott J. Goldman
  The QEMU runtime derives from QEMU and includes adaptations from UTM and
  Scott Goldman's Apple Silicon PCI passthrough work. The host app and
  DriverKit extension also derive in part from that work. See QEMU's
  LICENSE, COPYING and COPYING.LIB, and the notices in individual files.

The Linux kernel community and Fedora contributors
  The guest uses Linux and packages from a Fedora aarch64 builder. The
  Apple DMA, guest page pool and IVSHMEM modules retain their GPL and
  Linux syscall exception notices where applicable.

Device Tree Compiler and libfdt contributors
  The host launcher and QEMU use libfdt. See README.license and the source
  notices in the pinned DTC repository.

The Cornell Box
  The demo uses original procedural scene geometry and shaders inspired by
  the Cornell Box experiment from the Cornell University Program of
  Computer Graphics. It does not use Cornell's measured data or assets.

SDL, Khronos, glslang and libpng contributors
  The demo uses SDL3, the Vulkan loader and headers, glslang, and libpng.
  Refer to the license notices supplied by the corresponding packages.

NVIDIA is credited for the optional NVIDIA GPU driver and firmware.
Those artifacts are fetched from NVIDIA or supplied locally by the user
under their own terms. They are separate from this source distribution.
